// sokuji-native: read a fixed-size-element array in ONE call. Upstream loops
// read(dst[i]) per element, i.e. one read_raw of sizeof(T) bytes per element —
// through gguf_file_reader_callback that is one locked fread() per element. Every
// audio.cpp GGUF carries its sidecar files as a single UINT8 array KV
// (audiocpp.embedded_files.data: 57 MB for supertonic-3), and audio.cpp reopens a
// model GGUF 14 times per load, so supertonic paid ~800M one-byte freads = ~13.7s
// of the 14s load on the GB10. The guard is an INCLUSION list, not an exclusion
// list: only arithmetic T (uint8/16/32/64, int8/16/32/64, float, double — every
// type gguf_read_emplace_helper instantiates except bool and std::string) is
// read in bulk, so a future pin adding a type with a converting read() overload
// keeps the per-element loop by default instead of silently getting raw bytes.
// bool is arithmetic but excluded twice over: read(bool&) converts from int8 and
// std::vector<bool> is not contiguous. For the types that do qualify, std::vector
// stores them contiguously, so one read_raw over n*sizeof(T) bytes consumes
// exactly the same bytes in the same order and leaves data_offset / nbytes_remain
// identical; the n*sizeof(T) bound was checked above. The discarded if constexpr
// branch is never instantiated for std::string or bool.
if constexpr (std::is_arithmetic_v<T> && !std::is_same_v<T, bool>) {
    return read(static_cast<void *>(dst.data()), n * sizeof(T));
}
